meta {
  name: Controlled Task Availability Drill
  type: http
  seq: 6
}
put {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/companies/{{companyId}}/phase-routing
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}
auth:bearer {
  token: {{boardToken}}
}
body:json {
  {{completeCompanyPolicyWithDrill}}
}

docs {
  DO NOT SEND a partial example policy. This request intentionally requires a complete JSON variable;
  an unset variable is invalid JSON and cannot overwrite the policy.
  First run Get Company Defaults. Set completeCompanyPolicyWithDrill to the entire response.policy,
  preserving every field and existing task exception. Merge ONLY this field at taskOverrides[taskId]:
  {"availabilityDrill":{"id":"<fresh UUID>","phase":"planning","profileId":"<existing primary id>","expiresAt":"<UTC deadline>"}}
  Do not replace the company policy with this fragment. Keep planning, implementation, review, role
  responsibility maps, usage caps, quota settings and other exceptions from the GET response.
  Use a fresh drill UUID and an expiry within 30 minutes. taskId must identify an unassigned,
  unstarted backlog task in the company. After saving, assign/start that task normally.
  The primary profile is unavailable only for this exact task and phase. The ordinary fallback
  selector and all execution gates still apply. One activity receipt is recorded durably.
  No provider quota receipt, account cooldown, session reset or automatic retry is created.
  The deadline bounds first activation. An unactivated expired drill fails closed, with no
  renewal. A previously activated drill keeps its fallback affinity until the task is terminal,
  so the CTO can resume its saved session after a long implementation. The effective chain
  must match the full board-owned profile identities; agent overrides cannot replace it.
  After completion remove this task exception, preserving all other policy fields. Remove it
  before rolling back to a release whose strict policy schema predates availabilityDrill.
}
